insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('a2-f-001','aplus2','core',1,'1.1',$$What are the key differences between Windows Home and Pro editions?$$,$$Pro adds BitLocker, domain/Azure AD join, Group Policy, Remote Desktop host, and Hyper-V - the business management features Home lacks.$$),
('a2-f-002','aplus2','core',1,'1.2',$$Which Windows tool manages services, startup, users, and performance in one place?$$,$$Task Manager covers processes/startup/performance/users; services.msc manages services in depth; Computer Management bundles Event Viewer, Disk Management, and Users.$$),
('a2-f-003','aplus2','core',1,'1.3',$$gpupdate, sfc /scannow, chkdsk - what does each do?$$,$$gpupdate refreshes Group Policy. sfc /scannow verifies/repairs protected system files. chkdsk checks/repairs disk file-system errors (with /f) and bad sectors (/r).$$),
('a2-f-004','aplus2','core',1,'1.4',$$What is the difference between a quick format and a full format?$$,$$Quick: rewrites the file table only - data remains recoverable. Full: also scans for bad sectors and overwrites (Vista+), taking far longer.$$),
('a2-f-005','aplus2','core',1,'1.5',$$Which Windows networking settings matter for joining a domain?$$,$$Correct DNS (pointing at the domain controller), the computer account/credentials, network profile, and time sync - Kerberos fails if clocks drift over 5 minutes.$$),
('a2-f-006','aplus2','core',1,'1.7',$$GPT vs MBR partitioning?$$,$$GPT: up to 128 partitions, disks >2 TB, requires UEFI boot, has backup partition tables. MBR: legacy, 4 primary partitions, 2 TB limit, BIOS boot.$$),
('a2-f-007','aplus2','core',1,'1.8',$$What package managers install software on Ubuntu and macOS?$$,$$Ubuntu/Debian: apt (apt install). macOS: the App Store or Homebrew (brew install); .dmg images and .pkg installers for manual installs.$$),
('a2-f-008','aplus2','core',1,'1.9',$$Common Linux commands: ls, cd, cp, mv, rm, chmod, chown, sudo?$$,$$ls list, cd change dir, cp copy, mv move/rename, rm delete, chmod change permissions, chown change owner, sudo run as root. Also: grep search, ps processes, df disk space.$$),
('a2-f-009','aplus2','core',2,'2.1',$$Name the physical security controls A+ expects you to know.$$,$$Access control vestibules, badge readers, video surveillance, alarms, motion sensors, door locks, equipment locks, guards, bollards, fences, and lighting.$$),
('a2-f-010','aplus2','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the difference between authentication factors and give examples?$$,$$Something you know (password/PIN), have (smart card, authenticator app, key fob), are (fingerprint, face). MFA combines different factor types.$$),
('a2-f-011','aplus2','core',2,'2.5',$$NTFS permissions vs share permissions?$$,$$Share permissions apply only over the network; NTFS applies locally and remotely. Effective access is the MOST restrictive combination; NTFS permissions inherit down folders.$$),
('a2-f-012','aplus2','core',2,'2.5',$$What is the principle of least privilege in practice on Windows?$$,$$Users run as standard users, not administrators; elevation only when needed (UAC); admin accounts separate from daily accounts; permissions granted per role.$$),
('a2-f-013','aplus2','core',2,'2.6',$$Key Active Directory concepts: domain, OU, GPO, home folder?$$,$$Domain: security boundary of accounts/computers. OU: container for organizing objects. GPO: policy applied to OUs/domains. Home folder/roaming profile: per-user network storage.$$),
('a2-f-014','aplus2','core',2,'2.4',$$Common wireless security: WPA2 vs WPA3, PSK vs Enterprise?$$,$$WPA3 adds SAE (resists offline dictionary attacks). Personal/PSK: one shared passphrase. Enterprise: per-user 802.1X/RADIUS authentication - revocable individually.$$),
('a2-f-015','aplus2','core',2,'2.3',$$Malware types on Core 2: trojan, rootkit, ransomware, spyware, keylogger, boot sector virus?$$,$$Trojan: disguised as legit software. Rootkit: hides at OS/kernel level. Ransomware: encrypts for payment. Spyware/keylogger: covert data/keystroke capture. Boot sector: infects boot records.$$),
('a2-f-016','aplus2','core',2,'2.4',$$Social engineering: phishing, spear phishing, shoulder surfing, tailgating, evil twin?$$,$$Phishing: mass email lure. Spear phishing: targeted. Shoulder surfing: watching input. Tailgating: following through a secure door. Evil twin: rogue AP mimicking a real SSID.$$),
('a2-f-017','aplus2','core',2,'2.7',$$What data destruction methods does A+ distinguish?$$,$$Recycling-safe erasure: overwrite/secure wipe. Degaussing (magnetic media, destroys drive). Physical destruction: shredding/drilling/incineration - highest assurance, with certificates of destruction.$$),
('a2-f-018','aplus2','core',2,'2.8',$$Router hardening basics for a SOHO network?$$,$$Change default admin password, update firmware, use WPA3/WPA2, disable WPS, change default SSID, disable remote/WAN management, and consider a guest network.$$),
('a2-f-019','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$Windows slow performance - standard checklist?$$,$$Task Manager for CPU/RAM/disk hogs, startup programs, malware scan, disk space (10-15% free), Windows/driver updates, and failing-disk checks (S.M.A.R.T.).$$),
('a2-f-020','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$What are the go-to boot repair tools in Windows RE?$$,$$Startup Repair, bootrec (/fixmbr /fixboot /rebuildbcd), System Restore, safe mode, and command prompt. Access via Advanced Startup or install media.$$),
('a2-f-021','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$Standard malware removal steps in order (A+ 7-step)?$$,$$1 Identify symptoms. 2 Quarantine the system. 3 Disable System Restore. 4 Remediate (update AV, scan, remove). 5 Schedule scans/run updates. 6 Re-enable System Restore, create restore point. 7 Educate the user.$$),
('a2-f-022','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$Phone battery drains fast and runs hot - likely causes?$$,$$Rogue/misbehaving apps (check battery usage), poor signal boosting radio power, malware, old battery, high screen brightness, or excessive background sync.$$),
('a2-f-023','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$Browser redirects and unwanted pop-ups appear constantly. Diagnosis path?$$,$$Likely adware/PUP or malicious extension: remove suspicious extensions/programs, reset browser settings, run anti-malware, check proxy and DNS settings.$$),
('a2-f-024','aplus2','core',4,'4.1',$$What belongs in ticket documentation?$$,$$Problem description as reported, device/user details, troubleshooting steps taken, resolution, and time - clear enough that another tech can pick it up.$$),
('a2-f-025','aplus2','core',4,'4.2',$$Change management basics on A+: what precedes a change?$$,$$Request form, purpose, scope, risk analysis, rollback plan, sandbox testing, end-user acceptance, and change board approval before implementation.$$),
('a2-f-026','aplus2','core',4,'4.3',$$The 3-2-1 backup rule?$$,$$3 copies of data, on 2 different media types, with 1 offsite. Test restores regularly - an untested backup is a hope, not a plan.$$),
('a2-f-027','aplus2','core',4,'4.4',$$ESD prevention when working inside a PC?$$,$$Wrist strap and ESD mat, touch bare metal chassis, avoid carpet, keep parts in antistatic bags, manage humidity - static kills components silently.$$),
('a2-f-028','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$What is PII/PHI handling on A+ and why does it matter to a tech?$$,$$Personal/health data on machines you service is regulated (GDPR/HIPAA); don't browse it, report exposure, follow retention and chain-of-custody procedures.$$),
('a2-f-029','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$Proper communication and professionalism basics?$$,$$Be on time, avoid jargon, listen without interrupting, set and meet expectations, avoid distractions, maintain confidentiality, offer options and document everything.$$),
('a2-f-030','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What is a chain of custody in incident handling for techs?$$,$$A documented record of who had the evidence (device/data), when, and why - preserved intact when illegal content or a security incident is discovered; report through proper channels.$$),
('a2-f-031','aplus2','core',1,'1.6',$$Windows Update vs Feature Update vs Quality Update?$$,$$Quality updates: monthly cumulative security/bug fixes. Feature updates: annual version upgrades. Drivers/firmware can also ship via Windows Update.$$),
('a2-f-032','aplus2','core',2,'2.2',$$What does a screen lock policy on mobile devices enforce?$$,$$PIN/password/biometric requirement, auto-lock timeout, failed-attempt actions (wipe after N tries) - baseline defense for lost/stolen devices with remote wipe as backstop.$$),
('a2-f-033','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$Windows shows "Operating System Not Found" - top causes?$$,$$Boot order pointing at wrong device, corrupted bootloader/BCD, failed/disconnected drive, or BIOS/UEFI mode mismatch (GPT disk with legacy boot).$$),
('a2-f-034','aplus2','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the practical limit difference between 32-bit and 64-bit Windows?$$,$$32-bit addresses ~4 GB RAM max and can't run 64-bit apps. 64-bit supports vastly more memory and runs 32-bit apps via WOW64. New systems are 64-bit only.$$),
('a2-f-035','aplus2','core',2,'2.1',$$What is a mantrap/access control vestibule for?$$,$$Two interlocked doors that admit one authenticated person at a time - defeats tailgating into secure areas.$$),
('a2-a-001','aplus2','acronym',1,'1.3',$$SFC$$,$$System File Checker - verifies and repairs protected Windows system files.$$),
('a2-a-002','aplus2','acronym',1,'1.7',$$GPT$$,$$GUID Partition Table - modern partition scheme for UEFI, >2 TB disks.$$),
('a2-a-003','aplus2','acronym',1,'1.5',$$UAC$$,$$User Account Control - Windows elevation prompts enforcing least privilege.$$),
('a2-a-004','aplus2','acronym',2,'2.6',$$GPO$$,$$Group Policy Object - centralized Windows configuration applied via AD.$$),
('a2-a-005','aplus2','acronym',2,'2.6',$$OU$$,$$Organizational Unit - Active Directory container for organizing objects.$$),
('a2-a-006','aplus2','acronym',2,'2.5',$$NTFS$$,$$New Technology File System - Windows file system with permissions and encryption.$$),
('a2-a-007','aplus2','acronym',2,'2.4',$$WPA3$$,$$Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 - current wireless security standard with SAE.$$),
('a2-a-008','aplus2','acronym',2,'2.1',$$MFA$$,$$Multifactor Authentication - combining different factor categories.$$),
('a2-a-009','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.2',$$PUP$$,$$Potentially Unwanted Program - bundled adware/toolbars degrading systems.$$),
('a2-a-010','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.4',$$ESD$$,$$Electrostatic Discharge - static electricity that damages components.$$),
('a2-a-011','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.5',$$PII$$,$$Personally Identifiable Information - data identifying an individual.$$),
('a2-a-012','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.5',$$EULA$$,$$End User License Agreement - software usage license terms.$$),
('a2-y-001','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain the 7-step malware removal process and why System Restore is disabled mid-process.$$,$$Model answer: Identify symptoms, quarantine the machine (off the network), disable System Restore, remediate (update definitions, scan, remove), schedule future scans and updates, re-enable System Restore and make a clean restore point, then educate the user. System Restore is disabled because restore points can contain the malware - leaving them means a later restore reinfects the cleaned machine. You wipe the contaminated history, clean the system, then start fresh restore points from a known-good state.$$),
('a2-y-002','aplus2','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain how NTFS and share permissions combine when a user accesses a folder over the network.$$,$$Model answer: Two gates stand between a network user and the file. The share permission gate applies only to network access; the NTFS gate applies always. Within each type, permissions accumulate (most permissive of your groups wins, except explicit Deny). But BETWEEN the two types, the effective result is the MOST RESTRICTIVE: Full Control on the share with Read on NTFS nets you Read. Locally, only NTFS applies - which is why a user might modify a file at the machine but not over the network.$$),
('a2-y-003','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain the 3-2-1 backup rule and why each number exists.$$,$$Model answer: Keep 3 copies of your data (the original plus two backups) so no single loss is fatal. Store them on 2 different media types (say, internal disk and external drive or cloud) so one technology's failure mode doesn't take both. Keep 1 copy offsite so fire, theft, or ransomware at your location can't reach everything. Each number closes a specific failure scenario - and none of it counts until you've tested that a restore actually works.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('a2-q-001','aplus2',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A small business needs BitLocker and the ability to join a domain. Which Windows edition is the minimum?$q$,$q$["Windows Home","Windows Pro","Windows Media Edition","Windows Starter"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Pro adds BitLocker, domain/Azure AD join, Group Policy, and RDP hosting. Home lacks all of these business features.$q$),
('a2-q-002','aplus2',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Which command verifies and repairs protected Windows system files?$q$,$q$["chkdsk /r","sfc /scannow","gpupdate /force","diskpart"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$sfc /scannow checks system file integrity and repairs from the component store. chkdsk fixes disk errors, gpupdate refreshes policy, diskpart manages partitions.$q$),
('a2-q-003','aplus2',1,'1.7','mcq',2,$q$A new 4 TB drive must be a single bootable volume on a UEFI system. Which partition style?$q$,$q$["MBR","GPT","FAT16","Dynamic-only"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$GPT supports disks over 2 TB and is required for UEFI boot. MBR caps at 2 TB with 4 primary partitions; FAT16 is a file system, not a partition style.$q$),
('a2-q-004','aplus2',1,'1.9','mcq',2,$q$Which Linux command changes file permissions?$q$,$q$["chown","chmod","grep","ps"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$chmod modifies permissions (read/write/execute). chown changes ownership, grep searches text, ps lists processes.$q$),
('a2-q-005','aplus2',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Why is a quick format insufficient before donating a PC?$q$,$q$["It takes too long","It only clears the file table - data remains recoverable","It damages the drive","It removes the OS license"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Quick format rewrites the file allocation structures only; recovery tools can restore the data. Use a secure wipe (overwrite) or full format on modern Windows before disposal.$q$),
('a2-q-006','aplus2',1,'1.5','mcq',3,$q$A PC cannot join the domain and Kerberos errors mention time. What should you check?$q$,$q$["The mouse driver","Clock synchronization - Kerberos fails if time skew exceeds ~5 minutes","Monitor resolution","The printer spooler"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Kerberos authentication depends on synchronized clocks; drift beyond the tolerance (default 5 minutes) breaks domain authentication. Fix NTP/time settings first.$q$),
('a2-q-007','aplus2',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which tool shows which startup programs are slowing boot and lets you disable them?$q$,$q$["Task Manager (Startup tab)","Disk Management","Device Manager","Registry backup"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Task Manager's Startup tab lists autostart programs with impact ratings and enables/disables them. Disk Management handles volumes; Device Manager handles hardware.$q$),
('a2-q-008','aplus2',1,'1.8','mcq',2,$q$Which command installs software on Ubuntu from the terminal?$q$,$q$["brew install","apt install","winget install","dmg install"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Ubuntu/Debian use apt. Homebrew (brew) is macOS/Linuxbrew, winget is Windows, and .dmg is a macOS disk image format, not a command.$q$),
('a2-q-009','aplus2',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A user with 32-bit Windows wants to use 16 GB of RAM. What must happen?$q$,$q$["Nothing - 32-bit supports it","Install 64-bit Windows; 32-bit addresses only ~4 GB","Add a bigger page file","Enable ReadyBoost"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$32-bit Windows can address only about 4 GB. Using 16 GB requires a 64-bit OS (and CPU). Page files and ReadyBoost don't change addressable RAM.$q$),
('a2-q-010','aplus2',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Which command-line tool repairs the boot configuration data store?$q$,$q$["bootrec /rebuildbcd","format c:","tracert","tasklist"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$bootrec /rebuildbcd (from Windows RE) rebuilds the BCD when Windows won't boot. format erases, tracert traces routes, tasklist lists processes.$q$),
('a2-q-011','aplus2',1,'1.6','mcq',2,$q$What is the difference between a quality update and a feature update?$q$,$q$["No difference","Quality: monthly cumulative fixes; Feature: annual version upgrades","Quality updates are optional games","Feature updates only change wallpaper"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Quality updates are the monthly cumulative security/reliability patches; feature updates are the yearly Windows version upgrades with new capabilities.$q$),
('a2-q-012','aplus2',1,'1.9','mcq',2,$q$Which command shows your current directory contents on Linux?$q$,$q$["dir /w only","ls","cd","rm"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ls lists directory contents on Linux/macOS. cd changes directory, rm deletes. (dir works in some shells but ls is the standard answer.)$q$),
('a2-q-013','aplus2',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Which physical control specifically prevents tailgating into a data center?$q$,$q$["CCTV","Access control vestibule (mantrap)","A louder alarm","Cable locks"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A vestibule's interlocked doors admit one authenticated person at a time, directly countering tailgating. CCTV records, alarms alert, cable locks secure equipment.$q$),
('a2-q-014','aplus2',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Which combination is true multifactor authentication?$q$,$q$["Password + security question","Password + fingerprint","PIN + password","Two passwords"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Password (know) plus fingerprint (are) spans two factor categories. Questions, PINs, and passwords are all knowledge - stacking them is still one factor.$q$),
('a2-q-015','aplus2',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$A user has Full Control on a share but only Read via NTFS. What can they do over the network?$q$,$q$["Full control","Read only - the most restrictive combination applies","Nothing","Modify but not read"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Effective access across share and NTFS permissions is the most restrictive of the two: Read wins here. Locally, only NTFS would apply.$q$),
('a2-q-016','aplus2',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which wireless configuration gives each employee individual, revocable credentials?$q$,$q$["WPA2-PSK shared passphrase","WPA2/WPA3 Enterprise with RADIUS (802.1X)","Open network with MAC filtering","WEP"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Enterprise mode authenticates each user through RADIUS - one departure revokes one account. Shared passphrases require re-keying everyone; MAC filtering is spoofable; WEP is broken.$q$),
('a2-q-017','aplus2',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Malware that hides in the kernel and conceals other malicious processes is a:$q$,$q$["Trojan","Rootkit","Adware","Boot sector virus"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rootkits embed at the OS/kernel level to hide themselves and other malware from scanning. Trojans are disguised installers; adware shows ads; boot sector viruses infect boot records.$q$),
('a2-q-018','aplus2',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An attacker sets up a Wi-Fi network named identically to the coffee shop''s so victims connect through it. What is this?$q$,$q$["Bluejacking","Evil twin","Shoulder surfing","Whaling"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An evil twin is a rogue AP mimicking a legitimate SSID to intercept traffic. Bluejacking is Bluetooth spam, shoulder surfing is visual snooping, whaling is executive phishing.$q$),
('a2-q-019','aplus2',2,'2.7','mcq',2,$q$Drives with regulated patient data are being retired and must never be readable again, with proof. Best method?$q$,$q$["Quick format","Physical destruction (shredding) with a certificate of destruction","Deleting the files","Moving them to a locked drawer"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Regulated data leaving service warrants physical destruction plus a certificate documenting it. Formatting and deletion leave recoverable data; storage isn't destruction.$q$),
('a2-q-020','aplus2',2,'2.8','mcq',2,$q$Which set of steps hardens a SOHO router?$q$,$q$["Keep defaults for simplicity","Change default admin password, update firmware, use WPA3, disable WPS and remote management","Broadcast a hidden SSID only","Enable WEP for compatibility"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Router hardening: replace default credentials, patch firmware, use modern encryption, disable WPS (PIN brute-forceable) and WAN-side administration. Hiding SSIDs and WEP add no real security.$q$),
('a2-q-021','aplus2',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$Which Active Directory feature applies password and desktop policies to all computers in a department?$q$,$q$["Home folders","A GPO linked to that department's OU","BitLocker","Roaming profiles"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Group Policy Object linked to the department's Organizational Unit enforces settings on every object in it. Home folders and roaming profiles handle user data, BitLocker encrypts disks.$q$),
('a2-q-022','aplus2',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$Why should daily work be done from a standard user account rather than an administrator account?$q$,$q$["Standard accounts are faster","Least privilege - malware executed by a standard user cannot make system-wide changes without elevation","Administrators cannot browse the web","It saves licensing costs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Running as standard user contains damage: malware inherits your privileges, and without admin rights it can't easily modify the system. UAC elevation happens only when explicitly needed.$q$),
('a2-q-023','aplus2',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A user''s files are suddenly encrypted with a note demanding cryptocurrency. Which malware and which preparation defeats it?$q$,$q$["Spyware; longer passwords","Ransomware; tested offline/offsite backups","Adware; pop-up blocker","Keylogger; screen privacy filter"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Ransomware encrypts data for payment; recovery without paying depends on backups it couldn't reach (offline/offsite, tested). The other pairings mismatch threat and control.$q$),
('a2-q-024','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Windows boots to "Operating System Not Found". Which is the FIRST thing to check?$q$,$q$["Reinstall Windows immediately","Boot order/BIOS sees the drive, then repair the bootloader","Replace the monitor","Update the GPU driver"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Confirm firmware detects the disk and boot order targets it; then repair boot records (bootrec) from RE. Reinstalling first destroys data unnecessarily; display/GPU are unrelated.$q$),
('a2-q-025','aplus2',3,'3.2','ordering',3,$q$Order the CompTIA malware removal steps.$q$,$q$["Identify malware symptoms","Quarantine the infected system","Disable System Restore","Remediate: update anti-malware and scan/remove","Schedule scans and run updates","Re-enable System Restore and create a restore point","Educate the end user"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]$q$::jsonb,$q$The 7-step process: identify, quarantine, disable System Restore (infected restore points would reinfect), remediate, schedule scans/updates, re-enable restore with a clean point, educate the user.$q$),
('a2-q-026','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A PC takes 10 minutes to boot. Which is the BEST first diagnostic?$q$,$q$["Buy a new PC","Review startup programs and disk health (Task Manager, S.M.A.R.T.)","Reinstall the browser","Change the wallpaper"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Slow boots usually trace to bloated startup lists or a failing/full disk - check Task Manager's Startup tab and drive health before drastic steps.$q$),
('a2-q-027','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Every browser search redirects to an unfamiliar site and new toolbars appeared. What is the FIRST cleanup step?$q$,$q$["Buy a new keyboard","Remove suspicious extensions/programs and run an anti-malware scan","Disable the firewall","Switch ISPs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Redirects and toolbars indicate adware/PUPs or malicious extensions: uninstall them, reset browser settings, scan with anti-malware, and verify proxy/DNS settings.$q$),
('a2-q-028','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A phone''s battery suddenly drains in hours and the device runs warm. BEST first check?$q$,$q$["Replace the screen","Battery usage stats for a rogue app, then close/remove it","Factory reset immediately","Disable the lock screen"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Battery statistics reveal which app is burning power (often a stuck or malicious one); remove or update it. Factory reset is a last resort; screens and lock settings are unrelated.$q$),
('a2-q-029','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$Windows repeatedly blue-screens after a new video driver install. Which recovery path is BEST?$q$,$q$["Format the disk","Boot safe mode and roll back the driver (or use System Restore)","Replace the RAM first","Ignore it"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A crash starting right after a driver install points at that driver: safe mode loads minimal drivers so you can roll back, or System Restore returns to the pre-install state. Hardware swaps come only if that fails.$q$),
('a2-q-030','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Why is the user educated as the LAST malware-removal step?$q$,$q$["Punishment","Because behavior caused the infection and awareness prevents recurrence","It is legally required","To sell training"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Most infections arrive through user actions (attachments, downloads, fake alerts). Education closes the loop so the clean machine doesn't get reinfected the same way.$q$),
('a2-q-031','aplus2',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$What makes ticket documentation useful to the NEXT technician?$q$,$q$["Vague summaries","Clear problem description, steps already tried, and the resolution","Only the user's name","Emoji ratings"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Good tickets record the reported problem, environment, every step attempted, and the outcome - so work isn't repeated and patterns emerge across incidents.$q$),
('a2-q-032','aplus2',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Before a company-wide software rollout, which change management artifact reduces the risk of being stuck with a broken deployment?$q$,$q$["A marketing plan","A rollback plan","A new logo","Extra licenses"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The rollback plan documents how to revert if the change fails - required before implementation. Approval, risk analysis, and sandbox testing round out the change request.$q$),
('a2-q-033','aplus2',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$Which backup arrangement satisfies the 3-2-1 rule?$q$,$q$["Three copies on the same disk","Original + external drive copy + cloud copy","Two copies both onsite","One copy, compressed twice"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Original plus external drive (second media type) plus cloud (offsite) = 3 copies, 2 media, 1 offsite. Same-disk or all-onsite copies fail the rule's intent.$q$),
('a2-q-034','aplus2',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$Before replacing RAM, which precaution protects the components?$q$,$q$["Work on carpet in socks","Wear an ESD wrist strap and touch the bare metal chassis","Leave the PSU switched on","Rub the modules on wool"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Grounding via wrist strap and chassis equalizes potential, preventing electrostatic discharge that silently kills components. Carpet, wool, and energized systems increase risk.$q$),
('a2-q-035','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',3,$q$While repairing a PC you discover prohibited content. What is the correct action?$q$,$q$["Delete it quietly","Stop, preserve the evidence (chain of custody), and report through the proper channel","Confront the user","Copy it for proof to your own drive"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Discovering prohibited content requires stopping work, preserving the device/data intact with documented chain of custody, and reporting to the designated authority. Deleting or copying destroys/ mishandles evidence.$q$),
('a2-q-036','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$A frustrated customer vents while you diagnose. What is the professional response?$q$,$q$["Interrupt with the fix","Listen without interrupting, restate the problem, and set clear expectations","Match their frustration","Leave"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Active listening, restating the issue, and setting honest expectations de-escalate and build trust. Interrupting or mirroring frustration worsens the interaction.$q$),
('a2-q-037','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$A donated batch of office PCs may contain customer PII. What must happen before donation?$q$,$q$["Nothing - deleting the user profile suffices","Secure data sanitization (wipe or destroy drives) per policy","Just log out","Remove the RAM"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$PII demands verified sanitization - secure overwrite or drive removal/destruction per policy - before hardware leaves control. Profile deletion and logout leave recoverable data.$q$),
('a2-q-038','aplus2',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which utility views Windows logs to diagnose a service that fails at boot?$q$,$q$["Event Viewer","Paint","Snipping Tool","Character Map"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Event Viewer records system/application/security events including service failures with error details - the first stop for boot-time service issues.$q$),
('a2-q-039','aplus2',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Someone in line watches a user type their phone PIN. Which attack is this and a simple defense?$q$,$q$["Phishing; spam filter","Shoulder surfing; privacy screen and shielding input","Tailgating; mantrap","Evil twin; VPN"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Observing credentials being entered is shoulder surfing - defeated by privacy filters, shielding the input, and biometric unlock. The other pairs describe different attacks.$q$),
('a2-q-040','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A phone cannot install an app due to insufficient storage. Reasonable FIRST steps?$q$,$q$["Factory reset","Clear cache/unused apps and offload media, then retry","Replace the battery","Disable Wi-Fi"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Free space by clearing caches, removing unused apps, and offloading photos/videos. Factory reset is disproportionate; battery and Wi-Fi are unrelated to storage.$q$);